Most advice on late-night scrolling is about rules: keep the phone out of the bedroom, stop at a hard cutoff. That works for some people. Many of us still end up in bed with a tight jaw and a slightly quick heartbeat.

The research suggests the content matters more than the clock. In a study of 830 young adults, emotional investment and frequent checking predicted poor sleep more strongly than total screen time. The blue light effect is real but modest by comparison.

We wrote a calm, evidence-honest guide that includes a 60-second breathing script you can use in bed with the phone still in your hand, plus what breathing can and can't do for sleep. {url}
